// A bounded **type parameter in receiver position**: `T.m(…)` inside `fn build<T: Buildable>`.
//
// A bound licenses a trait's methods, and a method the trait declares `static` takes no receiver —
// so it is reachable on `T` exactly as it is on a concrete type. It was not: the receiver fell
// through to the value path and `T` was reported missing (`[E0005] cannot find 'T' in this scope`),
// at the DEFINITION site, which is the tell that name resolution simply never consulted generic
// scope. A turbofish at the call site did not help, because there was nothing wrong with the
// inference.
//
// `static` is what makes the call legal, and it is a **declaration**, not a survey: the trait says
// once that no implementation binds `self`, every `impl` is held to that (E0015), and the generic
// body is believed without a single implementor being consulted. Drop the modifier and the same
// call is E0047 — see `type_param_static_call_undeclared.noe` — because a self-less implementation
// today says nothing about the implementation written tomorrow.
//
// Generics are erased, so the compiled body holds no type to dispatch on — but the instantiation's
// NAME reaches it on the same per-instantiation channel `type_name::<T>()` reads, and by-name
// dispatch of a receiverless function is something the runtime already does. So one compiled body
// serves every `T`, and the two implementors below reach different code through the same call.
//
// Both parameter channels are exercised: a generic FN's own parameter (`build`/`tag_of`, on the
// hidden type-argument slot) and a generic TYPE's parameter inside an instance method (`Holder`,
// read off the receiver's reflected tag).
// expect: stdout "5"
// expect: stdout "thing"
// expect: stdout "box"
// expect: stdout "Box"
// expect: stdout "thing"
// expect: exit 0
trait Buildable {
    static fn make(a: int, b: string): Self
    static fn tag(): string
}
struct Thing {
    v: int
    impl Buildable {
        pub fn make(a: int, b: string): Thing { return Thing { v: a + b.len() } }
        pub fn tag(): string { return "thing" }
    }
}
class Box {
    n: int
    impl Buildable {
        pub fn make(a: int, b: string): Box { return Box { n: a } }
        pub fn tag(): string { return "box" }
    }
}
// `Self` in the trait's contract is `T` here, so the call satisfies the declared `T` return.
fn build<T: Buildable>(a: int, b: string): T {
    return T.make(a, b)
}
// A non-`Self` return through the same receiver.
fn tag_of<T: Buildable>(): string {
    return T.tag()
}
// A generic TYPE's parameter, inside an instance method — the other channel.
struct Holder<T: Buildable> {
    n: int
    pub fn describe(): string {
        // `self` is what makes this an INSTANCE method, which is what gives the class's `T` a
        // channel here: its name is read off the receiver's reflected type tag.
        held = self.n
        return T.tag()
    }
}
echo build::<Thing>(2, "abc").v
echo tag_of::<Thing>()
echo tag_of::<Box>()
// That `build::<Box>` really produced a `Box` is asked of the VALUE's own type tag. `tag` cannot
// answer it: it is declared `static`, which makes it type-only on a concrete receiver as well as
// reachable on `T` — `build::<Box>(…).tag()` is E0047 (`traits/static_trait_method_type_only.noe`).
echo type_of(build::<Box>(7, "z")).name()
h: Holder<Thing> = Holder { n: 1 }
echo h.describe()
